  "ground_truth": "on 20 July 2009",
  "final_output": "The first bank in India that was established to serve rural areas with basic banking and financial services was the **Punjab National Bank (Punjab National Bank Rural Bank)**, but more accurately, the **State Bank of India (SBI)** played a pivotal role in rural banking through its rural branch network.\n\nHowever, the first institution specifically established to serve rural areas was the **Grameen Bank**, founded in **1972** in Bangladesh — not India.\n\nIn India, the **first rural bank** established with a specific focus on rural financial services was the **Rural Bank of India**, but the correct and earliest recognized institution is the **State Bank of India's rural outreach**, which began in the 1960s.\n\nBut the most accurate answer to the original query is:\n\nThe **first bank in India specifically established to serve rural areas** was the **Punjab National Bank's rural branch network**, but the first dedicated rural bank in India was the **Rural Bank of India**, established in **1969**.\n\nHowever, the **first bank in India to focus on rural financial services** was the **State Bank of India**, which began its rural banking operations in the **1960s**, with the establishment of the **State Bank of India (SBI) Rural Bank** in **1969**.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\n**1969** — when the State Bank of India launched its rural banking initiative, marking the beginning of organized rural banking in India.\n\n✅ Final Answer: **1969**.",
